      {
        "id": "p6-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "What do you notice?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The difference of the squares of consecutive whole numbers equals their sum.",
          "worked": "Each pair of consecutive whole numbers differs by $1$. The difference-of-two-squares identity therefore gives their sum multiplied by $1$, which is their sum.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Use the factorisation of $(n+1)^2-n^2$ to prove the result of part b.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(n+1)^2-n^2=2n+1=n+(n+1)$, the sum of the consecutive whole numbers.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(n+1)^2-n^2&=(n+1-n)(n+1+n)\\\\(n+1)^2-n^2&=1(2n+1)\\\\(n+1)^2-n^2&=2n+1\\\\(n+1)^2-n^2&=n+(n+1)\\end{align*}$$\nThus the difference of their squares equals their sum.",
